<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nile</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nile is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, flowing north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its annual flood sustained the agricultural economy of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and made the valley one of the richest regions of the ancient and medieval world. It was the foundation of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>, and later supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg" alt="Nile" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fiktube · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
